          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stael-corinne-862bf46287163d7f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Corinne directly avows that she loves Oswald, presenting her love as a real and trustworthy state that grounds her moral judgment of him. Her reassurance and invitation to judge himself through her heart do not constitute a distinct relational undertaking. Although she cries out emotionally, the words themselves remain an emphatic avowal; the passage gives no independent indication that “je vous aime” is a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance contributes to intimate reassurance, but that interpersonal effect does not independently support performative force; “s’écria-t-elle” indicates intense delivery without by itself establishing expressive/reflexive force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Corinne directly says to Oswald, within narrated dialogue, “Je vous respecte autant que je vous aime.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Oswald has asked Corinne to judge whether he is morally responsible for his father’s death. Corinne invokes her respect and love as evidence that her exculpating assessment is trustworthy, then explicitly characterizes her love as sincerely felt and grounded in Oswald’s goodness and sensitivity. The declaration is passionate and consoling, but primarily truth-conditional rather than an undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je vous respecte autant que je vous aime: jugez-vous dans mon coeur; prenez-le pour votre conscience.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present avowal presents Corinne’s love as an existing emotional state and makes that state the basis for her judgment of Oswald.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ah! l'amour, tel que je le sens, n'est point une illusion”\",\"supports\":\"Corinne explicitly insists on the reality of love as she feels it, strongly reinforcing truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“c'est parce que vous êtes le meilleur, le plus sensible des hommes, que je vous admire et vous adore.”\",\"supports\":\"She supplies reasons and related attitudes that elaborate the avowed loving state rather than undertaking a new commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Non, s'écria-t-elle” introduces Corinne’s response.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes emotionally intense delivery, but supplies no independent evidence that the highlighted words function as a reflex-like discharge rather than an emphatic avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Chapter II of Corinne; ou, l'Italie.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identification and grammatical form of the classified occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its consoling purpose, passionate delivery, and use as moral testimony are contextual features that do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
